\\n  - **All Pungs?** No \\u2014 you\\u2019ll have two pungs and likely chows.  \\n  - But forming more structure increases chance to reach 8 points.  \\n- Risk of not acting: if you don\\u2019t Peng when able, you miss opportunity to build toward a structured hand.  \\n- However, consider tile visibility: B6 is heavily used (three copies already exposed: one kong/pung by across, one in chow by previous), B7 in two melds, so B8 may be rare \\u2014 better to secure the pung now.  \\n- Also, your hand is weak in flexibility \\u2014 grabbing B8 pung adds immediate value.\\n\\nReason:  \\nPeng B8 strengthens your hand structure significantly. It gives you an immediate exposed pung, works toward patterns like **All Pungs** (6 pts) or **Outside Hand** (if terminals/honors dominate), and uses a tile that is becoming scarce. You already have a pung in prevalent wind (F3), so adding another pung gets you closer to scoring thresholds. With W1-W2-W3 chow potential and possible J1 pair, you can aim for at least 8 points. Passing leaves you with fragmented tiles and low chance of improvement.\\n\\nAnswer: Peng B8\"}", "tile": "{\"system_prompt\": \"\\n    You are a Chinese Standard Mahjong (\\u56fd\\u6807\\u9ebb\\u5c06, or MCR Mahjong) player. It is a four-player game, and it is played with a basic set of 136 tiles with Chinese characters and symbols.\\n    Players draw and discard tiles in turn until they complete a winning hand with a 14th tile. The basic type of winning hand consists of four melds and a pair, while there exist winning hands with several special patterns.\\n    **Tiles and Representation**\\n    * There are 4 identical copies of each suited tile and honored tiles.\\n    * Tiles are represented using Letter+Number, i.e. W7, T2, B4, F1, J3, etc.  \\n    * Suited tiles are divided into 3 suits: Characters(\\u4e07), Bamboos(\\u6761), and Dots(\\u997c), each numbered from 1 to 9.\\n    * Letter 'W' represents Character suit(\\u4e07),  'T' for Bamboo suit(\\u6761), 