*cannot draw or expect**: W8, W9, B6, B8, B9, etc.\n\nMy possible discards: [Play B3; Play W3; Play W1; Play B5; Play B7; Play J1; Play W2; Play B2]\n\nLet\u2019s evaluate safety and strategic value:\n\n- **Play W1**: Terminal, safe to discard to remove terminal. Helps toward All Simples.\n- **Play W3**: Simple tile, but part of potential W1-W2-W3 chow? But W2 is in hand. I have W1, W2, W3 \u2014 that's a full chow! [W1,W2,W3] is a chow. Oh! I missed that.\n\nWait \u2014 hand: W1, W2, W3 \u2014 that\u2019s a complete chow! Why isn't it grouped?\n\nSo I actually have a concealed chow: [W1,W2,W3], plus B2,B3,B5,B7,J1.\n\nBut B2 and B3 could form a partial chow with B4 \u2014 but B4 only has 1 left, and someone just discarded B4 recently (me: Play B4 twice). And B4 is rare.\n\nAlso, B2,B3,B5,B7 \u2014 hard to connect.\n\nBut W1,W2,W3 is a clean chow \u2014 if I keep it concealed, I can use it later.\n\nNow, **All Chows** (2 points) is possible if I can form four chows and a pair of suited tiles. Currently, I have:\n- Concealed chow: W1-W2-W3\n- Could try to make chows from B2-B3-(B4) \u2192 but B4 only 1 left, and likely guarded.\n- Or B5-B7 \u2192 gap, needs B6 \u2192 B6 has 0 left \u2192 impossible.\n- B7 is isolated.\n\nJ1 is single dragon.\n\nAlternatively, **Half Flush**? Not really \u2014 too many suits.\n\n**Mixed Straight**? Need 123, 456, 789 in different suits. I have W123, but no 456 or 789.\n\n**Short Straight**? Two consecutive chows like 234+567 \u2014 not applicable.\n\nBest path: aim for **All Chows** (2 points) + **Prevalent Wind** (F3 pung, 2 points) + possibly **No Honors** if I discard J1? But J1 is in hand, not melded.\n\nWait \u2014 **All Chows** requires four chows and a pair \u2014 all must be chows, no pungs. But I already have two **exposed pungs** (F3 and B8). So I cannot go for All Chows, because I already have pungs \u2014 those are not chows.\n\nCrucial: **All Chows** pattern requires *all four melds to be chows*. But I have two pungs already exposed \u2014 so **All Chows is impossible**.\n\nSimilarly, any hand with pungs/kongs cannot count All Chows.\n\nSo that door is closed.\n\nTherefore, I must work with what I have: two pungs (F3, B8), and a mixed hand.\n\nPossible patterns:\n- **All Pungs** (6 points): I need four pungs/kongs. Currently have two. I have a chow (W1-W2-W3) \u2014 but that\u2019s not a pung. To get All Pungs, I\u2019d need to break the chow and instead form pungs elsewhere \u2014 but no pairs in hand.\n\nHand: W1,W2,W3,B2,B3,B5,B7,J1 \u2014 all singles except no pair.\n\nSo no pair currently \u2014 the pair will come from the winning tile.\n\nBut to make All Pungs, I need four pungs \u2014 meaning I must convert everything into triplets via pungs or kongs.\n\nCurrently:\n- Melds: F3 pung, B8 pung \u2192 2\n- Need two more pungs.\n\nPossible candidates: \n- J1 \u2014 could pung if someone discards J1\n- B2, B3, B5, B7 \u2014 all different, hard to pung\n- W1,W2,W3 \u2014 same\n\nUnlikely to form two more pungs.\n\nAlternatively, **Two Dragon Pungs** (6 points): need two dragon pungs. I have none yet. Only one J1.\n\n**Half Flush** (6 points): hand with one suit and honors. My melds are F3 (honor), B8 (dots), and hand has W (characters) and B (dots). So two suits + honors \u2014 possible if I can shift toward B and honors.\n\nBut I have W1,W2,W3 \u2014 characters \u2014 which would need to be discarded.\n\nBut I can\u2019t discard the whole set easily.\n\nBetter to minimize damage.\n\nBack to discards.\n\nKey: avoid dangerous tiles. What tiles are safe?\n\nFrom unshown tiles:\n- B6: 0 left \u2014 safe to discard? No \u2014 if 0 left, then others may be waiting on it, but since none exist, cannot win on it \u2014 so **safe**.\n- Similarly, W8: 0, W9:0, B8:0, B9:0, F1:0, F3:0, F4:0, J3:0 \u2014 all gone.\n\nSo discarding **B7**: B7 has 2 left \u2014 not safe \u2014 someone might be using it.\n\nB5: 2 left \u2014 possible.\n\nJ1: 2 left \u2014 could be used for dragon pung.\n\nW1: 1 left \u2014 risky? But W1 is terminal.\n\nBut look at other players\u2019 melds:\n\nNext player: [T4,T5,T6], [W6,W7,W8], [T3,T3,T3] \u2014 they have W6,W7,W8 \u2014 so W8 is used, W9 might be dangerous? But W9 gone.\n\nThey don\u2019t seem to need W1.\n\nAcross: [B6,B6,B6], [W5,W6,W7] \u2014 they have W5,W6,W7 \u2014 so W7 used, W8 used by others \u2014 W1 less likely.\n\nPrevious: [B6,B7,B8], [B9,B9,B9] \u2014 they have B6,B7,B8 \u2014 so **B7 is in their meld** \u2014 they formed a chow with B7 \u2014 so B7 is known to be useful to them \u2014 possibly still drawing?\n\nBut they already completed the chow \u2014 unless they want another pung.\n\nBut possible danger.\n\nAlso, in history, previous player played B3, B7 \u2014 wait, they played B7? No \u2014 \"Chi B7\" \u2014 means they used a discarded B7 to complete a chow. So yes, they claimed B7 \u2014 so B7 is somewhat public.\n\nBut since they already used it, maybe not waiting on it anymore.\n\nStill, better to avoid B7.\n\nSimilarly, B2: previous player played B2 \u2014 not claimed \u2014 so maybe safe.\n\nBut let\u2019s see who might be waiting.\n\nBest discard: one that is least useful, helps my hand.\n\nOption: **Play J1** \u2014 discard the dragon. Then I remove an honor, and if I can get rid of W1 and B7, I can approach **All Simples**.\n\nBut I still have F3 and B8 pungs \u2014 F3 is wind (honor), B8 is simple \u2014 so **All Simples impossible** due to F3 pung.\n\nHuge point: **All Simples** requires *no terminals or honors in the entire hand*. But I have a pung of F3 (honor) \u2014 so **All Simples is impossible**.\n\nSame for **No Honors** \u2014 I have F3 pung \u2014 so cannot have No Honors.\n\nSo those low-point patterns are blocked.\n\nBest I can do is score with existing structure.\n\nCurrent possible scoring elements:\n- **Prevalent Wind** (F3 pung) \u2192 2 points\n- **Pung of Terminals or Honors** \u2192 F3 and B8: B8 is not terminal (8 is not 1 or 9) \u2014 so only F3 counts? Wait: **Pung of Terminals or Honors**: pung of terminal (1 or 9) OR honor. F3 is honor \u2192 qualifies. B8 is neither terminal nor honor \u2192 does not qualify. So only one such pung \u2192 1 point.\n- Possibly **One Voided Suit** (1 point): I have characters (W), dots (B), and honors (F3). No bamboos in hand, but next player has T3,T3,T3 \u2014 I have no bamboo in hand or melds? Melds: F3, B8 \u2014 B8 is dots. Hand: W and B. So suits: W and B \u2014 two suits. Honors included. So three types? Suits: W (characters), B (dots), and honors. But **One Voided Suit** means exactly two of the three suits \u2014 i.e., missing one suit.\n\nHere, I have W and B, no T \u2014 so **missing Bamboo suit** \u2192 qualifies for **One Voided Suit** (1 point).\n\nYes.\n\nSo current minimum base: \n- Prevalent Wind: 2\n- Pung of Terminals or Honors: 1 (F3)\n- One Voided Suit: 1\nTotal: 4 points \u2014 but I need 8 to win.\n\nSo I need more.\n\nCan I get **All Pungs**? 6 points \u2014 if I have four pungs.\n\nCurrently two: F3, B8.\n\nIf I can form two more pungs from hand.\n\nBut hand has no pairs.\n\nUnless I keep a pair and wait to pung.\n\nFor example, if I keep J1, and someone discards J1, I can Peng.\n\nSimilarly, if I keep B2, and someone discards B2, I can Peng.\n\nBut speculative.\n\nAlternatively, **Mixed Double Pung**: two pungs in different suits with same number. But B8 and ? \u2014 no other 8.\n\nF3 is wind.\n\nNot helpful.\n\nAnother idea: **Outside Hand** (4 points): every meld and pair must consist of terminals or honors.\n\nCheck:\n- Meld 1: F3 pung \u2192 honor \u2192 OK\n- Meld 2: B8 pung \u2192 B8 is not terminal (8\u22601/9) and not honor \u2192 **invalid**\nSo B8 breaks Outside Hand.\n\nThus, **Outside Hand impossible**.\n\nOnly way to improve score is to aim for **All Pungs** (6) + **Prevalent Wind** (2) + **One Voided Suit** (1) + possibly **Concealed Pungs** if I form concealed ones \u2014 but I already have exposed.\n\nBut if I form two more pungs, even exposed, **All Pungs** is 6 points.\n\nWith Prevalent Wind (2), One Voided Suit (1), total 9 \u2014 enough.\n\nSo strategy: aim to form two more pungs.\n\nCandidates: J1, or B2/B3/B5/B7.\n\nB2: only 2 left \u2014 possible.\n\nBut B7: 2 left.\n\nJ1: 2 left.\n\nW1,W2,W3: each 1,4,3 left \u2014 possible to pung, but unlikely.\n\nBest chance: keep J1 \u2014 dragon tiles are often punged.\n\nOr keep a dot tile to match.\n\nBut I must discard something.\n\nNow, among valid actions:\n\n[Play B3; Play W3; Play W1; Play B5; Play B7; Play J1; Play W2; Play B2]\n\nDiscarding W1 removes a terminal, but doesn\u2019t help pung.\n\nDiscarding J1 removes chance for dragon pung.\n\nSince **Two Dragon Pungs** is 6 points, but I only have one dragon type, hard.\n\n**Dragon Pung** (2 points) if I pung J1.\n\nBut I only have one J1 now \u2014 if I discard it, I lose that chance.\n\nSo better not to discard J1.\n\nSimilarly, B2, B3, B5, B7 \u2014 if I keep one, I might form a pung.\n\nBut I must discard one.\n\nNote: I have W1,W2,W3 \u2014 a complete chow \u2014 but since I already have pungs, it\u2019s fine to keep as deadwood.\n\nBut if I discard one of them, I break it.\n\nBetter to keep the chow intact in case I need flexibility.\n\nBut it doesn\u2019t score anything extra.\n\nCompare safety:\n\n- **Play W1**: W1 has 1 left \u2014 someone might be waiting. Across player discarded W1 \u2014 so maybe not. Next player discarded W1 \u2014 also. So perhaps safe.\n- **Play W3**: W3 has 3 left \u2014 relatively common \u2014 safer.\n- **Play B3**: B3 has 2 left \u2014 previous player had B3 \u2014 they played B3 \u2014 so maybe not wanted.\n- **Play B5**: B5 has 2 left \u2014 no one has shown interest \u2014 safer.\n- **Play B7**: B7 was used by previous player in chow \u2014 riskier.\n- **Play J1**: J1 has 2 left \u2014 dragon \u2014 always risky, but possible target.\n- **Play W2**: W2 has 4 left \u2014 safest among W tiles \u2014 highest count.\n- **Play B2**: B2 has 2 left \u2014 previous player played B2 \u2014 so maybe safe.\n\nBest discard: one that is least useful to others and least valuable to me.\n\nSince I want to potentially form a pung, I should keep J1 (dragon) or a B tile.\n\nBut I have four B tiles: B2,B3,B5,B7 \u2014 if I discard one, say B5, I still have B2,B3,B7 \u2014 not helpful.\n\nNo pair.\n\nSo no strong reason to keep any specific B tile.\n\nJ1 is more likely to be punged than a random B tile.\n\nSo better to keep J1.\n\nAmong W tiles, W2 is most abundant (4 left), so **Play W2** is safe.\n\nW1 is terminal, but only 1 left \u2014 might be dangerous.\n\nW3: 3 left \u2014 medium.\n\nBut W2 is safest.\n\nAlso, discarding W2 breaks the chow W1-W2-W3 \u2014 but since I can\u2019t use it for All Chows anyway (due to pungs), and it doesn\u2019t score, breaking it is acceptable.\n\nAfter discarding W2, I have W1 and W3 \u2014 not useful.\n\nBut I could later pung them? Unlikely.\n\nAlternatively, discard a B tile.\n\n**Play B5**: B5 has 2 left, not involved in any shown meld. No one has B4-B6 or similar. Across has W5, but not B5. So probably safe.\n\n**Play B3**: previous player played B3 \u2014 so likely safe.\n\nBut **Play W2** seems safest.\n\nMoreover, W2 is not terminal, not honor, and high availability.\n\nFinal decision: discard **W2** to minimize risk while maintaining flexibility.\n\nAnswer: Play W2\n\nBut wait \u2014 is there a better option?\n\nWhat about **Play B5**? B5 is in the middle, could be part of 456, but B4 and B6 are scarce (B4:1 left, B6:0) \u2014 so no chow possible. Safe.\n\nBut W2 is safer (4 left vs B5:2).\n\nSimilarly, J1:2, but more valuable.\n\nSo **W2** is best.\n\nEvaluation: The safest discard is W2, as it has the highest remaining count (4 left) and is not involved in any opponent's recent melds. Discarding it does not hinder potential scoring patterns significantly, as the W1-W2-W3 chow cannot contribute to All Chows due to existing pungs. Keeping J1 offers a chance for a Dragon Pung (2 points), and preserving dot tiles allows for potential pungs. Other discards like B7 or J1 carry higher risk of aiding opponents.\n\nReason: W2 is the safest tile to discard based on availability and game history, minimizing the risk of feeding an opponent while maintaining options to develop pungs in dots or dragons.\n\nAnswer: Play W2"}
